// How the peoples pass on what they know (V2.1 §11): how readily the young take a technique from
// those who know it — the simple quickly, the deep slowly, since a skill resting on others is
// copied less faithfully and a small population cannot keep what too few can pass on (Henrich
// 2004; the Tasmanians' losses, Jones 1977) — how much less the grown learn, how far neighbours'
// knowers count, how often one tries something new, how much teaching speeds learning, how often
// the old tell stories in the evening.
(
    schema: 1,
    entries: [
        (
            id: "human_learning", name: "How our kind passes on what it knows", status: Implemented,
            species: "homo_sapiens",
            learn_year: 0.15, depth_factor: 0.9,
            learn_ages: (5.0, 25.0), grown_factor: 0.3,
            contact_weight: 0.25,
            innovate_month: 0.002, innovate_insight: 0.2,
            // Real teaching (V2.1 §11.2): shown and corrected, several times faster than watching.
            teach: 4.0,
            story_second: 0.002,
        ),
        (
            id: "australopith_learning", name: "How australopiths pass on what they know", status: Implemented,
            species: "australopithecus",
            // By watching alone, over years, as young chimpanzees learn to crack nuts (Biro et al.
            // 2003); no teaching to speak of, no stories.
            learn_year: 0.12, depth_factor: 1.2,
            learn_ages: (2.0, 12.0), grown_factor: 0.1,
            contact_weight: 0.1,
            innovate_month: 0.0003, innovate_insight: 0.1,
            teach: 1.0,
            story_second: 0.0,
        ),
    ],
